window.FFGuide="<article class=\"manual-document\" aria-label=\"FIRST FIRE user manual\">\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">FIRST FIRE · User manual · November 2026</p><header><h1>From a claim to a source</h1><p class=\"manual-sub\">Use this research desk to check what a space fire experiment actually measured, compare it with a mission environment, and decide what evidence you still need.</p></header>\n<p class=\"links\"><a href=\"index.html#atlas\">Open the Atlas</a><a href=\"first-fire-user-manual.pdf\" download>Download this guide as a PDF</a><a href=\"index.html#home\">Open the research desk</a><a href=\"technical-manual.html\">Technical manual</a></p>\n<h3>Your first three minutes</h3>\n<p>Suppose someone reports that a piece of Nomex fabric burned for 140 seconds on a spacecraft. The source has a 140-second entry. What was burning?</p>\n<ol><li>Open <a href=\"index.html#case/saffire\">Cases → Saffire-II</a>. In <strong>Evidence</strong>, read the question about Nomex ignition. The experiment exposed two adjoining materials, PMMA (acrylic) and Nomex, to a flame.</li>\n<li>Open the <a href=\"index.html#data?study=PSI-99&amp;q=2-7\">data explorer for sample 2-7</a>. Select the test number <strong>2-7</strong> to expand the original NASA row. The table lists “PMMA &amp; Nomex” and a burn time of 140 s. That duration belongs to the assembly.</li>\n<li>Read Note 2 in the <strong>Original NASA table (local CSV)</strong>, or open the investigator source on the case. The PMMA was consumed. The Nomex did not ignite in this test. Documented blackening alone cannot establish fabric ignition.</li>\n<li>Return to the case and open <strong>My research brief</strong>. Write which component ignited and one limit on applying this finding to a different atmosphere. Select <strong>Download brief</strong> to keep the finding and its citations together.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"case-note\"><strong>What changed?</strong> A true number supported the wrong claim when it was attached to only one component. Recording the sample, component and endpoint gives someone else a way to check your interpretation.</div>\n<h3>Opening the project</h3><p>Unpack the complete download, open its dashboard <code>index.html</code> in a current browser, and keep the adjacent files together. Search, original CSV tables, habitat ranking and practice work offline. External NASA pages and ordinary web search need an internet connection. The Atlas covers 1,622 source records in 16 tables from 15 investigations. The Data explorer covers 1,058 individually normalized observations. The extended catalog includes 19 investigations. The four-view walkthrough is on page 7.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\">Worked example source · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.60555/2chp-m469\">NASA PSI-99, v5, experimental table, sample 2-7 and Note 2.</a></p></section>\n\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">Research workflow · Evidence and decisions</p><h2>Find the right evidence</h2>\n<h3>Start with a question</h3><p>Use <strong>Find evidence</strong> on the Research desk for a subject such as “smoke from wire insulation.” Search ranks the published cases and investigation descriptions using a local semantic model and keyword matching. If model loading fails, the page identifies the keyword search. For an individual sample number, use <strong>Data explorer</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Follow a case</h3><p>Read the <strong>Observed record</strong>. In <strong>Evidence comparison</strong>, choose a suggested question or select <strong>Ask your own question</strong>. In the SAME case, try “Did the detector miss Teflon smoke?” Read the matching evidence and open its source. If the case cannot support your question, the panel explains the gap. The <strong>Research decision</strong> below gives the authors’ interpretation and next test. <strong>Six design options</strong> compares their proposed approaches.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Case directory\"><thead><tr><th>Open a case</th><th>The distinction to look for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/sibal\">SIBAL</a></td><td>Flame spread speed versus time to visible extinction.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/saffire\">Saffire-II</a></td><td>Damage to a fabric versus ignition of that component.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/flex\">FLEX</a></td><td>Loss of visible flame versus the end of chemical heat release.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/same\">SAME</a></td><td>Measured smoke particles versus reaching an alarm threshold.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/cfig\">CFI-G</a></td><td>Oxygen at the burner versus oxygen in the surrounding gas.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"index.html#case/ices\">ICES</a></td><td>A short partial-gravity observation versus an extrapolated limit.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Keep a usable brief</h3><p>In <strong>My research brief</strong>, enter the decision, one limitation and the next measurement or reanalysis. <strong>Save on this device</strong> stores your answers in the current browser profile. <strong>Download brief</strong> exports plain text and the case's primary sources. Use the download for sharing or for a durable copy before clearing browser storage.</p>\n<h3>Check your own wording</h3><p>Open <a href=\"index.html#claim\">Claim review</a>, enter your question and choose <strong>Find relevant cases</strong>. Select the experiment you mean. Map your endpoint, material, gravity and intended assertion to the source. A mismatch or unknown condition produces an insufficient-support finding. Read the source locator and proposed next measurement, then save or download the review. Choose <strong>No applicable case</strong> to record a gap.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\"><a href=\"index.html#sources\">Sources &amp; methods</a> includes the <a href=\"index.html#coverage\">Coverage map</a> (choose a subject and counted field), <a href=\"index.html#referee\">Referee</a> (test a quote, its interpretation and a supported number) and <a href=\"demo.html\">Project walkthrough</a>. A matching quotation establishes where the words occur; you decide what they establish.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">Data workflow · Observations and comparisons</p><h2>Inspect a NASA record</h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"index.html#data\">Data explorer → Observations</a>, choose an investigation or subject. Search for a test ID, material or endpoint. Click a column heading to sort; click again to reverse it. <strong>Next</strong> and <strong>Previous</strong> move through the results. Click the test number to see the unmodified row and the fields FIRST FIRE interpreted.</p>\n<h3>Two oxygen fields can look identical</h3><p>Choose <strong>CFI-G · PSI-159</strong>. Set minimum O₂ to <strong>34</strong> with the initial ambient oxygen field selected. You should find <strong>113</strong> observations. Switch the oxygen field to <strong>O₂ in burner stream</strong>. You should now see <strong>106</strong> different observations. An inverse burner feeds oxygen into a chamber containing fuel. An 85% burner stream does not describe an 85% habitat atmosphere. Choosing final oxygen asks another question again.</p>\n<h3>Trace or export</h3><p><strong>Export filtered CSV</strong> downloads every matching normalized record, across all pages. <strong>Original NASA table (local CSV)</strong> downloads the versioned archive table. The row locator counts the header as row 1. The DOI opens the investigation, and the versioned download identifies the upstream source file. The catalogue also links source tables that have no normalized observations.</p>\n<div class=\"case-note\"><strong>Read the missing fields.</strong> An em dash or blank means the field is unavailable, inapplicable or excluded for a stated reason. A recorded zero is a value. Fan display, smoke inlet velocity and burner mass flow refer to different quantities. When an oxygen interval is given, the numeric filter includes records whose intervals overlap your selection.</div>\n<h3>Compare with a habitat</h3><p>Open <strong>Rank for a habitat</strong>. Choose a scenario and required subject. Adjust gravity, ambient oxygen, absolute pressure and optional material text. Each investigation contributes its closest eligible record. Rows covering all three conditions rank ahead of rows with two or one, and the table names the missing fields. Inverse gas-burner oxygen is excluded from atmospheric comparisons.</p>\n<p>Expand <strong>Weights and distance calculation</strong> to change which differences matter. The sensitivity panel doubles each weight separately and shows whether the leading investigation order changes. A close score still leaves questions about orientation, sample size, ventilation and the observed endpoint. The orbit rows use nominal zero g.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\">Comparison source · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.60555/cyt1-kh64\">NASA PSI-159, v1, normal and inverse experimental tables.</a></p></section>\n\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">Prediction workflow · Assumptions and scoring</p><h2>Read the FM² forecast</h2>\n<p>Open <a href=\"index.html#forecast\">FM² forecast</a> to explore four planned fabric burns at 8.2 psia. The forecast asks whether flame keeps spreading after the igniter switches off. The fixed forecast has a signed timestamp dated 14 November 2026. You can edit a separate scenario. No lunar outcomes have been verified.</p>\n<h3>Change the assumptions</h3><p>Under <strong>Assumed oxygen limit</strong>, set the mean threshold and its spread in percentage points, or choose <strong>Set the central 80% interval directly</strong> and <strong>Use these bounds</strong>. The curve and the chances in <strong>Four planned burns</strong> update with your assumptions.</p>\n<h3>Read the fabric illustration</h3><p>Select a starting oxygen level. At the default 25% O₂, the two views show the same vertical 2 × 20 cm Synergy Pro Navy strip under different assumed limits. Choose <strong>Play ignition</strong> to replay the bottom wire heating and switching off. With the default bounds, 25% exceeds the lower limit (24.08%), so the front advances upward in that view. It falls below the upper limit (27.92%), so the other view shows no sustained spread. Changing a burn or an assumption updates both views. The full distribution gives a 25.25% chance at 25% O₂. Flame speed and travel distance are illustrative.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Illustrative forecast inputs and outputs\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Initial oxygen</th><th scope=\"col\">37%</th><th scope=\"col\">33%</th><th scope=\"col\">29%</th><th scope=\"col\">25%</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Default chance of sustained spread</th><td>&gt;99.99%</td><td>&gt;99.99%</td><td>97.72%</td><td>25.25%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3>Choose a follow-up measurement</h3><p><strong>Choose the next oxygen test</strong> compares one test or a fixed pair. A yes/no result near a 50% prediction can narrow the estimated threshold. Igniter reliability, sample count and platform time also affect test choice.</p>\n<h3>Interpret a score</h3><p>In <strong>Score hypothetical outcomes</strong>, mark each burn yes, no, pending or void. Both forecasts use the same resolved trials. Their Brier scores average the squared difference between each probability and observed outcome (1 or 0). Lower is better. The locked file specifies 90/90/90/10% for the project comparator. NASA supplied qualitative expectations. To score a future burn, verify the sample, atmosphere, igniter and outcome in a flight record. Keep interrupted or unreported trials out of the score.</p><p><strong>Export this scenario</strong> downloads your edited inputs and displays their checksum. Compare them with the <a href=\"https://first-fire-lunar-lab.christsamuel26.chatgpt.site/forecast-lock/fm2-v1.json\">fixed forecast</a> and its <a href=\"https://first-fire-lunar-lab.christsamuel26.chatgpt.site/forecast-lock/fm2-v1.tsr\">signed timestamp receipt</a>. The fixed file also gives illustrative travel lengths of 20, 17.5, 7.5 and 0 cm at the mean limit.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\"><a href=\"https://first-fire-lunar-lab.christsamuel26.chatgpt.site/technical-manual#fm2\">Read the full FM² chapter</a> · <a href=\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20260001966\">NASA experiment design, LPSC 2026</a></p></section>\n\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">Evaluation workflow · Hand-off and results</p><h2>Run a blind comparison</h2>\n<p>Open <a href=\"index.html#study\">Blind test</a>. <strong>Practice with six published cases</strong> lets you learn the controls with known material. Practice files are excluded from study results. To measure a benefit, a facilitator supplies six previously unseen questions and keeps the independently authored assessor key private, with credit criteria agreed before use.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Study roles\"><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Facilitator</td><td>Assign a unique anonymous slot from 1–12. Keep the assessor key private. Give participants equivalent source access, instructions and time limits. Supply only the participant pack. Across a run, each person uses FIRST FIRE for three cases and ordinary search for three.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Participant</td><td>Choose the assigned slot and <strong>Import facilitator’s participant pack</strong>. Select <strong>Reveal prompt and start timer</strong>, then use the assigned method. Enter a decision, a primary citation and a limit. The optional budget question asks what to do next and why. Submit or let the deadline record the attempt.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Assessor</td><td>Load the assessor key and <strong>one masked response file</strong>. Score the decision, citation and scope separately. Score a budget choice only where the study defines its rubric. Export grades. Keep the analyst file with the facilitator so assignment and timing stay hidden.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Analyst</td><td>Import the complete analyst records and the matching final grade files together. Review fully supported answers, correct sources, timeouts and median time. The optional budget measure has its own denominator. Export scores by case and participant.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Before leaving the device</h3><p>The active participant run uses local browser storage. The deadline continues while you consult sources or leave the page. At the end select <strong>Export masked responses</strong> and <strong>Export analyst record</strong>. Export grades before leaving the assessor page. A partial record is available under <strong>Session controls</strong> after at least one completed response. Export before clearing the session or switching devices.</p>\n<div class=\"case-note\"><strong>What is a real result?</strong> A completed facilitated run, matched to independently scored responses. This edition includes the protocol and practice materials. It has no published participant comparison. Report the number of people, missing sessions and uncertainty alongside any future result.</div>\n<p class=\"folio\"><a href=\"research/evaluation.md\">Study protocol</a> · <a href=\"research/practice/participant.example.json\">Rehearsal participant pack</a>. Facilitators can find its example key in the full source project at <code>research/evaluation-examples/key.example.json</code>. Use a new private key for an authentic study.</p></section>\n<section class=\"guideblock\"><p class=\"section-label\">Research chat</p><h2>Ask, follow up, then check the source</h2>\n<p>Choose <a href=\"index.html#chat\">Chat</a> in the top bar and enter your question. Open <strong>Explore</strong> for the case library, NASA records and benchmark.</p>\n<h3>Try a two-turn conversation</h3>\n<ol><li>Ask: “In Saffire-II sample 2-7, which material actually burned?” Open the cited flight report and check its sample number.</li><li>Follow up: “Does the 140 seconds belong to the Nomex?” Check whether the answer separates the whole assembly from the fabric. If it does not, the linked source takes priority.</li><li>Try a question the archive cannot settle: “What oxygen threshold is safe for my new spacesuit fabric?” A nearby material or a model probability cannot certify that fabric.</li></ol>\n<h3>Read or revisit a reply</h3><p>Select a numbered citation to open its source and check the measured conditions or the investigators’ findings. Choose <strong>Retry answer</strong> to try the same question in a separate chat. Refreshing the page opens an empty chat. Open <strong>History</strong> in the same browser tab to return to a previous conversation.</p>\n<h3>Start or reopen the local model</h3><p>From the complete project, follow the setup steps in README and start the local server. Open <strong>http://127.0.0.1:8765/#chat</strong> in your browser. A double-click on dist/index.html opens the archive edition instead. Set up model files once; an API key is not required for the local test.</p>\n<h3>Check an answer or change its reading level</h3><p>For supported archive questions, open <strong>Check this answer</strong> to inspect its checks. In the <a href=\"index.html#referee\">Referee</a>, select <strong>Test the planted quote</strong> and read the rejection reason. The <a href=\"index.html#referee?view=errors\">public error log</a> lists release examples. Your text stays on your device. In <strong>Reading preferences</strong>, pick an answer depth or use a local .txt or .md résumé to suggest one. You can override that suggestion.</p>\n<h3>Inspect mistakes</h3><p>Use <a href=\"index.html#benchmark\">the 100-question benchmark</a>. Choose “Failed a check,” open a question, and compare the returned answer and citations with the reference. These software scores do not establish human research performance, universal safety or that a simulation was run.</p></section>\n<section class=\"guideblock\" id=\"deck-workflow\"><p class=\"section-label\">Team Code Blooded · Dashboard walkthrough</p><h2>Follow a question across four views</h2>\n<h3>The Gap · Which tests were actually flown?</h3>\n<p>Open <a href=\"index.html#atlas\">Atlas</a>. Select a gravity and starting-oxygen cell, then open a source row. A zero is the count for that selection. The diamond marks a planned FM² starting condition. Select SPICE in the investigation filter to see why its 469 burner settings cannot be counted as 469 separate burns. <strong>Download CSV</strong> includes all matching records and their source locators.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"index.html#compass\">Compass</a>, choose a habitat and test subject. Check gravity, ambient oxygen and pressure for the closest test. In Atlas, open <strong>Compare two source records</strong> and select the chamber/burner pair. Only the chamber value describes the test atmosphere. Open each original row to see where NASA reported it.</p>\n<h3>The Audit · Does the claim transfer?</h3>\n<p><a href=\"index.html#rulebook\">Rulebook</a> checks seven questions across orbit, Moon and Mars, covering Earth screening, ventilation, specimen size, visible extinction, smoke detection, burn duration and burner oxygen. Select a rule and read its counterexample or unresolved condition. Open an <a href=\"index.html#passports\">investigation passport</a> for the source version, scope and original CSV.</p>\n<h3>The Oracle · What would change the prediction?</h3>\n<p>Open the <a href=\"index.html#forecast\">four-burn forecast</a> and follow page 4. Use the what-if controls to score a hypothetical outcome and find the next oxygen level to test. Return to the signed forecast when comparing a future official result.</p>\n<h3>At Home · What else must be measured?</h3>\n<p><a href=\"index.html#earth\">At Home</a> relates the evidence to crew cabins, oxygen wards and confined spaces. For Gaganyaan, check both total pressure and oxygen fraction. For a hospital drape, ask about oxygen near the actual fabric and ignition source. Follow each source for the conditions of that example.</p>\n<h3>Test a cabin design</h3>\n<p>In <a href=\"index.html#physics\">Physics Lab</a>, set a material, cabin volume, oxygen, pressure, spread speed and oxygen cutoff. Move the time control to read O₂, CO₂ and heat. In the <a href=\"index.html#physics?mode=rover\">rover sandbox</a>, select or name a cabin, chassis and battery. Under <strong>Reverse the flow</strong>, select <strong>Compare both inputs</strong>. The Saffire-I rates give 10.8 and 7.8 cm after 60 seconds with the default cabin. These distances are model outputs.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\"><a href=\"https://first-fire-lunar-lab.christsamuel26.chatgpt.site/technical-manual#engines\">Engine assumptions and verification</a> · <a href=\"https://first-fire-lunar-lab.christsamuel26.chatgpt.site/#referee\">Try a source check in the Referee</a></p></section>\n<section class=\"guideblock\" id=\"guided-investigation\"><p class=\"section-label\">Guided investigation</p><h2>Run the four-step demonstration</h2>\n<p>Open <a href=\"index.html#session\">Run the investigation</a> on the home page, enter your question, and choose <strong>Start guided session</strong>. The thin navigation bar above each tool moves you forward or back to your research record.</p>\n<h3>1. Compare NASA experiments</h3><p>In <strong>Matched experiments</strong>, keep the default methanol comparison and reveal the endpoints. The three burn durations change in different directions. Require the same test day and inspect how many pairs survive. A change in the average alone would miss this weakness.</p>\n<h3>2. Isolate a sensor</h3><p>In <strong>Tracer transport</strong>, change from default exchange to isolated compartments. The source receives the same tracer, but the remote sensor never crosses its threshold. Increase circulation and scrub the timeline. Compare the fitted time with a Berkeley Lab repeat. Under <strong>Fit a sensor trace</strong>, load two local CSVs or try the synthetic pair. Train on the first run, then inspect the errors on the second.</p>\n<h3>3. Design a lunar follow-up</h3><p>Start at 26% oxygen under the default assumptions. Record a hypothetical <strong>Spread sustained</strong> reading and inspect the next setting. Undo, record <strong>No sustained spread</strong>, and compare the other branch. Try optimizing the decision for a particular planned burn.</p>\n<h3>4. Ask the data and save your work</h3><p>Ask “How many methanol observations are in FLEX?” Then ask for their mean burn duration. Inspect the reported and missing measurements and download the calculation receipt. Use <strong>Review session → Download research record</strong> to keep the question, current inputs, results and sources together.</p>\n<p class=\"folio\"><a href=\"index.html#report/advanced-methods\">Methods and limitations</a> · <a href=\"index.html#report/science-chat-audit\">Independent chat questions</a></p></section>\n</article>";
